<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Wix xmlns="http://wixtoolset.org/schemas/v4/wxs"
     xmlns:bal="http://wixtoolset.org/schemas/v4/wxs/bal"
     xmlns:util="http://wixtoolset.org/schemas/v4/wxs/util">
  <!--
    SysManage Agent - WiX Bundle (Burn)

    WHY A BUNDLE EXISTS AT ALL
    ==========================
    The agent needs Python 3.9+ and the VC++ 2015-2022 runtime.  Neither can be
    installed from inside the agent MSI: both are Windows Installer packages,
    the MSI holds the Windows Installer mutex while its custom actions run, and
    a nested install is refused with 1618 (ERROR_INSTALL_ALREADY_RUNNING).
    Chaining prerequisites is exactly what Burn is for - it installs each
    package in its own transaction, in order, so Python is genuinely present
    before the agent MSI's custom actions look for it.

    THE MSI STILL HAS TO STAND ALONE
    ================================
    This bundle is the better experience, but it is a SEPARATE artifact that
    someone has to choose to download.  winget installs the MSI, `msiexec /i`
    installs the MSI, and the Pro+ provisioning path installs the MSI.  So the
    MSI keeps its own deferred-bootstrap scheduled task (bootstrap-task.ps1)
    rather than relying on this.  Where both apply, the bundle wins the race:
    Python is already installed by the time the MSI runs, check-python.ps1's
    detect-only pass is satisfied, and no task is ever registered.

    PREREQUISITES ARE EMBEDDED, NOT DOWNLOADED
    ==========================================
    Deliberate, and it costs ~55 MB.  SysManage supports air-gapped estates as
    a first-class scenario, and a bundle that reaches out to python.org mid-
    install is useless in exactly the environments that most need a single
    self-contained installer.  It also removes the most common cause of the
    original failure: no route to python.org.  build-bundle.ps1 fetches both
    payloads at BUILD time and verifies each one's Authenticode signature and
    signer before packaging; the Python payload can additionally be pinned by
    SHA-256, but the VC++ one cannot, because its aka.ms URL always serves the
    current build.
  -->

  <Bundle Name="SysManage Agent"
          Manufacturer="SysManage"
          Version="$(var.VERSION)"
          UpgradeCode="7F3A9C21-4B6E-4E2A-9D18-6C5A2E7B10D4"
          IconSourceFile="sysmanage-agent.ico"
          Compressed="yes">

    <BootstrapperApplication>
      <bal:WixStandardBootstrapperApplication
          Theme="hyperlinkLicense"
          LicenseUrl="https://www.gnu.org/licenses/agpl-3.0.html"
          SuppressOptionsUI="yes" />
    </BootstrapperApplication>

    <!--
      Is a usable Python already here?

      The agent's floor is 3.9, not 3.12, so this checks every interpreter we
      would accept rather than only the one we ship.  Installing a second
      Python onto a machine that already has a perfectly good 3.11 would be
      rude, slow, and a support burden when two interpreters disagree about
      which one owns the PATH.

      Per-machine installs only (HKLM): the agent runs as a SYSTEM service, so
      a per-user Python in some administrator's profile is not reachable by it
      and must not count as satisfying the prerequisite.
    -->
    <util:RegistrySearch Id="Python39"  Root="HKLM" Key="SOFTWARE\Python\PythonCore\3.9\InstallPath"  Result="exists" Variable="HavePython39" />
    <util:RegistrySearch Id="Python310" Root="HKLM" Key="SOFTWARE\Python\PythonCore\3.10\InstallPath" Result="exists" Variable="HavePython310" />
    <util:RegistrySearch Id="Python311" Root="HKLM" Key="SOFTWARE\Python\PythonCore\3.11\InstallPath" Result="exists" Variable="HavePython311" />
    <util:RegistrySearch Id="Python312" Root="HKLM" Key="SOFTWARE\Python\PythonCore\3.12\InstallPath" Result="exists" Variable="HavePython312" />
    <util:RegistrySearch Id="Python313" Root="HKLM" Key="SOFTWARE\Python\PythonCore\3.13\InstallPath" Result="exists" Variable="HavePython313" />

    <!-- VC++ 2015-2022 runtime, needed by the cryptography wheel. -->
    <util:RegistrySearch Id="VcRuntimeInstalled"
                         Root="HKLM"
                         Key="SOFTWARE\Microsoft\VisualStudio\14.0\VC\Runtimes\$(var.VcArch)"
                         Value="Installed"
                         Result="value"
                         Variable="VcRuntimeInstalled" />

    <Chain>
      <!--
        1. Visual C++ runtime.

        3010 is success-with-reboot-pending and must not be treated as failure;
        the agent does not need the reboot to have happened, and failing here
        would strand the whole chain over a cosmetic condition.
      -->
      <ExePackage Id="VcRedist"
                  DisplayName="Microsoft Visual C++ 2015-2022 Redistributable"
                  SourceFile="$(var.VcRedistPath)"
                  Permanent="yes"
                  Vital="yes"
                  Compressed="yes"
                  InstallArguments="/install /quiet /norestart"
                  DetectCondition="VcRuntimeInstalled = 1"
                  InstallCondition="NOT (VcRuntimeInstalled = 1)">
        <ExitCode Value="3010" Behavior="success" />
      </ExePackage>

      <!--
        2. Python.

        InstallAllUsers=1 because the agent runs as a SYSTEM service and a
        per-user interpreter would be invisible to it.  PrependPath=1 because
        the agent's own scripts locate the interpreter through PATH.

        Permanent="yes": uninstalling the agent must not remove a Python that
        other software on the box may since have started depending on.  Leaving
        an interpreter behind is a much smaller sin than breaking an unrelated
        application.
      -->
      <ExePackage Id="Python"
                  DisplayName="Python $(var.PythonVersion)"
                  SourceFile="$(var.PythonPath)"
                  Permanent="yes"
                  Vital="yes"
                  Compressed="yes"
                  InstallArguments="/quiet InstallAllUsers=1 PrependPath=1 Include_test=0 Include_launcher=1"
                  DetectCondition="HavePython312"
                  InstallCondition="NOT (HavePython39 OR HavePython310 OR HavePython311 OR HavePython312 OR HavePython313)">
        <ExitCode Value="3010" Behavior="success" />
      </ExePackage>

      <!--
        3. The agent itself.

        By the time this runs, Burn has committed the packages above in their
        own transactions, so check-python.ps1's detect-only pass finds Python
        and the MSI finishes the venv and the service in-transaction - no
        deferred task, and the service is running when the bundle returns.
      -->
      <MsiPackage Id="SysManageAgentMsi"
                  SourceFile="$(var.MsiPath)"
                  Vital="yes"
                  Compressed="yes" />
    </Chain>
  </Bundle>
</Wix>
